  "headline": "Ice berg melts, ocean level remains the same",
  "description": "highlights a common misconception about the impact of melting ice on sea levels. When an ice berg, which is already floating in the ocean, melts, it does not cause a significant rise in sea level. This is due to Archimedes' principle, which states that a floating object displaces its own weight in water. Therefore, the volume of water displaced by the ice berg is equal to the volume of water it will add when it melts, keeping the sea level relatively unchanged. However, this principle does not apply to land-based ice, such as glaciers and ice sheets, which, when melted, do contribute to rising sea levels as they add extra water to the oceans."
}

