  "points": [
    {
      "title": "Heat Sink",
      "description": "The oceans act as a massive heat sink, absorbing approximately 90% of the excess heat generated by greenhouse gas emissions. This thermal absorption is crucial because it moderates the rate at which the atmosphere warms, thereby influencing global temperature patterns. The oceans' capacity to store heat is vast, and this thermal inertia means that the effects of warming can be delayed, sometimes by decades or even centuries. This delayed response complicates our understanding of climate change impacts and necessitates long-term monitoring and predictive modeling."
    },
    {
      "title": "Carbon Sink",
      "description": "The oceans are a significant carbon sink, absorbing about 25% of the carbon dioxide (CO2) emitted by human activities. This absorption plays a critical role in mitigating the greenhouse effect, as CO2 is a potent greenhouse gas. However, the absorption of CO2 also leads to ocean acidification, which poses a serious threat to marine ecosystems, particularly to organisms with calcium carbonate shells and skeletons, such as corals and certain plankton species. The acidification process can disrupt marine food chains and biodiversity, with potential cascading effects on global climate regulation."
    },
