{
  "text": "The global retreat of glaciers is a phenomenon that has been extensively documented and studied by the scientific community, with overwhelming evidence pointing to the significant role of human-induced climate change. This retreat is not just a local or regional issue but a global one, affecting glaciers in various parts of the world, from the Andes to the Himalayas, and from the European Alps to the glaciers in Alaska. The rapidity of this retreat is alarming, as it not only impacts the local ecosystems and communities but also has broader implications for global water resources and sea-level rise.",
  "ipcc": {
    "description": "The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), a leading international body for assessing the science related to climate change, has been instrumental in compiling and disseminating this scientific evidence. The IPCC reports are comprehensive assessments of the state of knowledge on climate change, its impacts, and future risks, as well as options for adaptation and mitigation. These reports are based on the latest scientific findings and are reviewed by hundreds of experts from around the world, ensuring a high level of accuracy and reliability.",
